WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.800
Okay. The one other thing I want to talk about styling,

00:03.800 --> 00:06.640
which most people don't know about when it comes to Vue 3,

00:06.640 --> 00:07.880
is that this is great.

00:07.880 --> 00:10.380
We have the ability to global styles,

00:10.380 --> 00:13.200
we have scope styles, we have CSS modules.

00:13.200 --> 00:16.600
For me, one of the things that when it comes to extending

00:16.600 --> 00:20.080
on the functionality of the web is how we can

00:20.080 --> 00:22.600
bring the technology together in a better way.

00:22.600 --> 00:24.840
JavaScript is really good at interactivity,

00:24.840 --> 00:27.680
managing state, and dynamic pieces of information.

00:27.800 --> 00:30.440
CSS is very good at what it does, styling.

00:30.440 --> 00:35.080
So what if you could find a way to actually merge the two together in a way

00:35.080 --> 00:38.680
that allows for just crazy possibility?

00:38.680 --> 00:39.920
To show you what I mean,

00:39.920 --> 00:42.640
let's go ahead and I'm just going to save this right now.

00:42.640 --> 00:47.320
On the app.vue, what if we wanted the ability for the user to

00:47.320 --> 00:49.320
choose what the background color is?

00:49.320 --> 00:51.760
Typically, the approach for that is you probably would come to

00:51.760 --> 00:52.880
your designers and you would say,

00:52.880 --> 00:55.840
okay, these are like the seven classes we'll support,

00:55.840 --> 00:58.320
and so we'll try our best to just support it this way,

00:58.320 --> 00:59.840
and so you're limited.

00:59.840 --> 01:01.840
But there are plenty of cases where you might actually

01:01.840 --> 01:04.200
want to give the user infinite configuration,

01:04.200 --> 01:06.000
but the reason why we can't do that is because,

01:06.000 --> 01:08.600
frankly, managing the CSS for that can be quite difficult.

01:08.600 --> 01:11.240
So let's say for instance,

01:11.240 --> 01:13.520
we have that property.

01:13.520 --> 01:15.880
So I'm going to import a ref from view,

01:15.880 --> 01:18.920
and I'm going to call it color preference.

01:18.920 --> 01:20.800
It'll be a ref, it'll be an empty.

01:20.800 --> 01:23.160
Actually, we'll just say black for now.

01:23.160 --> 01:28.200
So what we'll do here is what you ideally want to do is you'd

01:28.200 --> 01:31.720
like your CSS to be able to read from your JavaScript.

01:31.720 --> 01:33.640
That would be a perfect world, wouldn't it?

01:33.640 --> 01:36.200
Right now, what you probably have to do is some convoluted

01:36.200 --> 01:38.540
like onload the document element,

01:38.540 --> 01:40.640
then insert your own style tag and manipulate

01:40.640 --> 01:42.760
the inline styles of the HTML.

01:42.760 --> 01:44.680
There's a lot of work that goes into that.

01:44.680 --> 01:50.000
Well, what if I said instead that what we could do is,

01:50.000 --> 01:52.400
let's say on the app itself,

01:52.400 --> 01:54.840
this time we're going to do a background color,

01:54.840 --> 01:58.560
but this time I'm going to actually,

01:58.560 --> 02:00.720
let's see, let's try to just do this.

02:00.720 --> 02:02.640
Will color preference work?

02:02.640 --> 02:04.720
No, nothing's happening.

02:04.720 --> 02:05.880
Well, let's make sure it's working first.

02:05.880 --> 02:06.920
So let's go ahead and do this.

02:06.920 --> 02:10.120
Let's actually render out the color preference.

02:10.120 --> 02:13.360
So color preference, where is it?

02:13.360 --> 02:15.680
It is, oh, it's all the way down here.

02:15.680 --> 02:17.760
Let's bump it up so it's easier to read.

02:17.760 --> 02:20.440
Okay, great. It is showing up.

02:20.440 --> 02:21.480
Let's make sure it actually can get

02:21.480 --> 02:23.120
changed that it's reactive.

02:23.120 --> 02:24.400
Let's do an import.

02:24.400 --> 02:27.120
If you didn't know, there's a type called color on

02:27.120 --> 02:29.200
your inputs that actually built into HTML,

02:29.200 --> 02:32.340
and we can just V-model this to our color preference.

02:32.340 --> 02:33.560
So let's see if this works.

02:33.560 --> 02:35.840
Let's see, let's go this. Okay, great.

02:35.840 --> 02:38.240
So now we have this infinite color loop.

02:38.240 --> 02:40.920
Well, view three was like,

02:40.920 --> 02:43.200
you know what, I think we can make this happen.

02:43.200 --> 02:47.280
What if we just wrap this in V-bind?

02:47.280 --> 02:55.360
So we get, shoot,

02:55.360 --> 02:59.080
do I need to, maybe app is too far of a context?

02:59.080 --> 03:01.760
There we go. Okay, that context was too high.

03:01.760 --> 03:06.040
All right, it's fine. I'll just do the, let me do this.

03:06.040 --> 03:07.400
Let me wrap this real quick.

03:07.400 --> 03:09.680
Okay, so I'll explain why that's not working real quick.

03:09.680 --> 03:11.400
It actually does make sense.

03:11.400 --> 03:15.880
But for the sake of showing this, there we go.

03:16.000 --> 03:20.280
Okay. We can see here now is you

03:20.280 --> 03:24.200
have this infinite loop of JavaScript updating the CSS.

03:24.200 --> 03:25.840
Now, if you're skeptical, you're like,

03:25.840 --> 03:28.280
this looks like black magic, what's happening here?

03:28.280 --> 03:29.920
All right, let me show you.

03:29.920 --> 03:32.200
Let me show you.

03:32.200 --> 03:35.240
I promise you, when we think about things,

03:35.240 --> 03:37.640
we try to think of things in terms of extending the web.

03:37.640 --> 03:39.880
Look at actually what's happening here.

03:39.880 --> 03:42.600
What view does, what JavaScript does well is it says,

03:42.600 --> 03:45.200
okay, I'm going to create a custom variable,

03:45.200 --> 03:47.540
but we're going to use CSS variables because guess what?

03:47.540 --> 03:50.360
That is browser technology that is actively supported,

03:50.360 --> 03:52.720
and why would we try to reinvent the wheel?

03:52.720 --> 03:55.420
All of JavaScript's doing is actually just

03:55.420 --> 03:58.920
tracking this variable and just making sure to update that value.

03:58.920 --> 04:00.800
But the rest of it is CSS.

04:00.800 --> 04:03.040
So you get the power of CSS in

04:03.040 --> 04:05.160
Cascades and everything in its native form,

04:05.160 --> 04:06.720
but you also get the power of getting

04:06.720 --> 04:09.200
reactive data that can impact those things.

04:09.200 --> 04:11.280
So you can imagine the number of

04:11.280 --> 04:12.960
times I've been on apps where I wanted to customize

04:13.040 --> 04:15.360
the font size or the padding.

04:15.360 --> 04:17.300
Those are hard to do because to create a class

04:17.300 --> 04:18.640
for every single one of those scenarios or to

04:18.640 --> 04:21.380
manage that at one point was nigh impossible.

04:21.380 --> 04:25.360
But with things like vbind on your styles now,

04:25.360 --> 04:29.000
oh, so many possibilities now exist with this.

04:29.000 --> 04:30.640
So if you're wondering though,

04:30.640 --> 04:32.560
why did I have a little bit of trouble with

04:32.560 --> 04:36.080
the HTML and the app is because I think

04:36.080 --> 04:40.840
it view once it takes over the div is unable to go outside of it.

04:40.840 --> 04:41.840
So that's what it is.

04:41.840 --> 04:42.880
It's just not in control,

04:42.880 --> 04:44.560
like view is not in control of that area.

04:44.560 --> 04:45.400
So if you're going to do this,

04:45.400 --> 04:47.800
obviously make sure that you're within the context of your app,

04:47.800 --> 04:49.640
otherwise the CSS won't apply.

04:49.640 --> 04:50.760
But otherwise at this point,

04:50.760 --> 04:53.400
you can imagine just infinite user preference configuration

04:53.400 --> 04:54.600
that's possible with this.

